gromacs~master~Iaea914a474195f214ca860f7345f6878b9a04813;d7ef7219a30f538d600919b7ea9634d1507c6cd4;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_gather.cu;325;0;0;        const float3* __restrict__ gm_coordinates = rVecBufferAsFloat3(kernelParams.atoms.d_coordinates)_;make d_coordinates a float3 and avoid the issue? _  _ If the approach is to use float3 near the device and RVec at high level, then it's reasonable to draw the line around where we set up the values of parameters to use in the device kernels. Thus d_coordinates and friends as float3 makes sense. However, we need to separate the lifetime-managing DeviceBuffer<RVec> objects (that are hiding device-side float3/cl_mem) from things we actually use in a kernel parameter struct (a float3* for CUDA, perhaps an offset for OpenCL). The latter has to be updated on the host when we reallocate the DeviceBuffer, and that is the natural point to do the cast. CUDA kernels will get a float3 and be happy. _  _ I have such a change for pme_gpu_types.h, which I can prepare for upload.
