gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;fa05692026dc043e42440d057769cad0b053d75f;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;73;0;0;    const auto  foundEntry = table_.insert(StringTablePair(stripped, size))_;This makes the index depend on the order the string are being inserted. Why? I thought we wanted it depend on the order of names. _  _ Note that if you use the insert order as the index you don't need the two tables. You could directly insert into the  finalTable_ (using push_back) because you anyhow sort the string by insert order not by sorted by name.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;fa05692026dc043e42440d057769cad0b053d75f;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;73;0;0;    const auto  foundEntry = table_.insert(StringTablePair(stripped, size))_;The possible issues I see with using unsorted storage: _ - For serialization either you need to do the sorting then or the serialization will depend on insert order which seems bad _ - If you end up needing findEntryByName after finalizing that will be very slow if unsorted. _  _ But if neither is an issue for what's planed than that's fine. _  _ If you wanted to directly insert into the final storage you could use a std::deque instead of std::vector. That doesn't invalidate any references after inserting and also O(1) random access. Of course in that case you can't have sorted storage without using indirect storage (because sorting obviously invalidate directly stored elements). So you would still need the unordered_map if you want fast findEntryByName for the initial phase. But you wouldn't need finalize anymore (it could still empty the unordered_map if we want).
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;fa05692026dc043e42440d057769cad0b053d75f;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;73;0;0;    const auto  foundEntry = table_.insert(StringTablePair(stripped, size))_;As far as I can see the usage of the old symbol table, there is no findEntryByName after serialization, so it "should"(tm) be fine. _  _ I see the point for the sorting. The issue is that at least the positions that the current StringTableEntry objects hold need to be valid in either the initial or finalized datastructure, so that we can actually store and write them again. _  _ This would go away when using the std::deque as you pointed out (and I totally forgot about it), _ and we could just read and write directly to that. _  _ But the fast lookup is still needed for insertion in the end, because there we might want to add and lookup strings for a few thousand to millions of atoms, so I would stick to the map for it.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;fa05692026dc043e42440d057769cad0b053d75f;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;73;0;0;    const auto  foundEntry = table_.insert(StringTablePair(stripped, size))_;This is keeping with the old implementation that also kept the index as the order in which the strings are inserted. I can change this so that the index is only used later from the finalized table. No real reason for me doing it like this here besides keeping it close to the legacy implementation. _ I can't use the final table directly because I still need the references to the strings valid during the time when strings are added in preprocessing, and as far as I can read the iterator/reference invalidation table they would not be valid once the capacity changes in the vector.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;fa05692026dc043e42440d057769cad0b053d75f;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;79;0;0;    if (tableIsFinalized_);Why not allow lookup by name before? Just not needed and thus not implemented? If so that's fine but should be a comment that it could be added and isn't disallowed by design. If it is disallowed by design than there should be a comment about the reason.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;fa05692026dc043e42440d057769cad0b053d75f;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;79;0;0;    if (tableIsFinalized_);I didn't see the reason for it, because lookup by name should only be needed when populating the table (if at all). Once the table is finalized all access should be index into the vector storage that is not implemented here. Changed it to say that this is not implemented instead.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;fa05692026dc043e42440d057769cad0b053d75f;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;113;0;0;    tableIsFinalized_ = true_;It looks to me like you don't need the table_ anymore after finalizing. If so you should free the memory.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;fa05692026dc043e42440d057769cad0b053d75f;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;113;0;0;    tableIsFinalized_ = true_;Done
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;bf645df11fa453ef3fbfa404395d3ab51bf14db3;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;69;0;0;    return &(*table_.insert(gmx::stripString(name)).first)_;If you follow my suggestion this beomes _   _  return table_.insert(gmx::stripString(name)).first->c_str()_
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;bf645df11fa453ef3fbfa404395d3ab51bf14db3;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;72;0;0;SymbolTableEntry SymbolTable::findEntryByName(const std::string &name) const;Do we need this function at all?
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;bf645df11fa453ef3fbfa404395d3ab51bf14db3;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;72;0;0;SymbolTableEntry SymbolTable::findEntryByName(const std::string &name) const;it is needed for const access :(
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;bf645df11fa453ef3fbfa404395d3ab51bf14db3;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;58;0;0;using SymbolTableStorage = std::set<std::string>_;Why did you change it to ordered? You are not using iterators anymore, thus it can't be the iterator invalidation issue. So what is it?
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;bf645df11fa453ef3fbfa404395d3ab51bf14db3;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;61;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = const std::string *_;I think it may be wiser to return const char * in the near term. Clients will have to change when they contain a std::string, but there's no real advantage to changing them to point at a std::string they don't own vs the current state of pointing at a C-string they don't own.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;bf645df11fa453ef3fbfa404395d3ab51bf14db3;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;61;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = const std::string *_;I opened #2916 to continue
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;bf645df11fa453ef3fbfa404395d3ab51bf14db3;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;61;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = const std::string *_;The end goal is to eliminate the container of strings and store the chars alongside the other data while we modernize other stuff. So the intermediate state of the code could either be wrapped container returning either string* or char* for convenience. There's no clear use for string_view in the end (though if we thought the optimization of out of line storage is useful, then it would have a role)
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;bf645df11fa453ef3fbfa404395d3ab51bf14db3;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;61;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = const std::string *_;Legacy symtab complicates the code (several data structures need one, or want want for full functionality_ that complicates code that wants to copy and/or modify objects of gmx_mtop_t or t_atoms and friends). It does provide a space optimization (at run time and in the .tpr, though the former competes with DSO). It's unclear what performance characteristics it has (individual strings probably better as SSO but as those are repeated often, maybe overall cache footprint is better with the symtab_ serialization performance is likely dominated by the sheer number of small things to handle). _  _ We could introduce the sorted vector of strings in this patch. It seems overall better than any other thing we could do now. Then we can perhaps decide better whether it plus string_view makes sense. Paul's probably best placed to decide how awkward e.g. t_atoms becomes if you still have to work with it in the context of a symtab maintained in a gmx_mtop_t.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;bf645df11fa453ef3fbfa404395d3ab51bf14db3;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;61;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = const std::string *_;Why? What do we gain by replacing the  SymTab with storing the strings directly. How is short-string optimization better than the current optimization? It seems always worse (as you pointed out about the tpr size and the MPI issue).  And I don't see what this simplifies if we had a modern SymTab. _  _ I think we should keep the SymTab idea and then we should make the new one serializable. And neither set nor unordered_set is good for serialization. Set doesn't provide an efficient way to get the position and unordered_set doesn't have a position. _  _ It would be easy to create a modern SymTab which is efficient and easy to use. The linked-list storage format of the legacy symtab isn't a good solution because isn't efficient (lookup by name shouldn't be O(N) and linked-list is bad for cache)  nor is is convenient (there is no need why one has to provide the index or the pointer rather than a normal string for lookup, especially if lookup is made fast).  _  _ I think a good implementation would be a sorted vector. Especially if we are OK with having  finalizeTable. Then we simply do push_back for enterString (amortized constant), sort for finalizeTable (O(NlogN)) and use std::lower_bound for lookup (O(log(N)). If we want to avoid having finalizeTable we would need to replace enterString with lower_bound+insert (O(N)). There would be a couple of other good container options if for some reason this simple sorted vector isn't sufficient. _  _ But before deciding on the container type we should decide whether we really want to get rid of the SymTab idea.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;bf645df11fa453ef3fbfa404395d3ab51bf14db3;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;61;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = const std::string *_;I disagree. It seems obvious that the correct choice would be compat::string_view. But until we have that I think string* is best. Because that still allows us to use the STL like interface (member function and operators for string and our stringutil for std::string). Then when we change it to string_view all we need to do is change it from pointer to value but otherwise it would stay the same. I don't see any advantage of using char*.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;bf645df11fa453ef3fbfa404395d3ab51bf14db3;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;61;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = const std::string *_;In general I don't mind the idea of having a central place to look up small strings that are duplicated many times for a large system, because it drastically reduces the storage size needed for all information. Also, I think the optimization question is a moot point, as the only time we care about lookup/search times in the symbol table is during preprocessing (meh), file reading (once) and communication (once during setup). After that, the table is const and makes it possible to just dereference the single pointer to the string that is stored, and we don't care about lookup times any more because there is no lookup, but only the pointer dereference. _  _ I can see the advantage of not using the symtab at all as well, and instead storing all strings explicitly. This can be done easily in the modernized datastructures, and file reading/communication is also not an issue then as we can serialize the string directly while reading/writing the datastructure. Disadvantage is then that all old TPR files will be unreadable, if we don't use the legacy datastructure as an intermediate. And new files might become excessively large for systems that contain all information in a single molecule type. _  _ Concerning the serialization, we could switch to a custom vector of string, with the push_back/emplace_back function hidden behind a wrapper that checks if the entry is already there, similar to solutions we have for preprocessing atom types and residuetypes. This would mean we could use the positions in the vector for serialization instead.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;bf645df11fa453ef3fbfa404395d3ab51bf14db3;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;86;0;0;         * \returns New unique iterator to entry in storage, or iterator to existing entry with matchin name.;needs update. Isn't iterator anymore.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;81f142b33b2bc40af2271efb6952bd943f76bf59;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;182;0;0;    //! Find matching entry in storage by name as string.;The documentation should specify what the return value is and what happens when the string is not found. _ Currently this method throws when the string is not found. Is that useful? Wouldn't we rather want to return that it is not found, e.g. by returning -1?
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;81f142b33b2bc40af2271efb6952bd943f76bf59;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;182;0;0;    //! Find matching entry in storage by name as string.;It is a user error to search for a string that has not been added to the StringTable, so throw makes sense. The main 'user' will be preprocessing code where such a throw will likely indicate that there is a typo in a force field or structure file.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;1c1297dcf1b312a432894f37bc9dd9e4ef01b26b;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;114;0;0;    auto found = std::find_if(table_.begin(), table_.end(),;This looks like complexity O(#keys). The find method of the map has constant complexity.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;1c1297dcf1b312a432894f37bc9dd9e4ef01b26b;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;114;0;0;    auto found = std::find_if(table_.begin(), table_.end(),;the corresponding functions in t_symtab are inly used for file reading/writing and during the initial bcast to the nodes, so I'm not sure how big of an performance hit this would give. The current code always accesses the elements directly, so with this it would just access the iterator
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;1c1297dcf1b312a432894f37bc9dd9e4ef01b26b;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;132;0;0;        const SymbolTableEntry enterString(const std::string &name)_;We should consider enabling inlining htis.
