gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;5907524046c75e4b9f01b07a368cbd1903c6fea2;src/gromacs/mdlib/broadcaststructs.cpp;184;183;184;void StringTable::distributeTableStorage(const t_commrec *cr);This looks a bit misplaced here, though I understand the reasoning that this file collects all the broadcasting I would prefer have it with the other StringTable function implementations.  _  _ An alternative pattern that might be preferable, is to add a serialization function to a final StringTable and a constructor that builds a StringTable from ISerializer. _  _ Then, the StringTable does not need to be aware of commrec and it can be even better tested.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;ab8f9bf180a103e47c1aea6e2338ca716bffc5f9;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;73;0;0;{;Is it supposed to be allowed to call this method if tableIsFinalized_ is false? If not you should check for that. If so you will need a gurantee that rehashing cannot occur if you want to return either a pointer or iterator. Because right now the insert called from enterString could cause a rehash which would invalidate the pointer/iterator returned from this.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;ab8f9bf180a103e47c1aea6e2338ca716bffc5f9;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;73;0;0;{;see my comment in the header
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;ab8f9bf180a103e47c1aea6e2338ca716bffc5f9;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.cpp;73;0;0;{;Again didn't read properly. Only iterators but not pointers are being invalidated: "If rehashing occurs due to the insertion, all iterators are invalidated. Otherwise iterators are not affected. References are not invalidated. Rehashing occurs only if the new number of elements is greater than max_load_factor()*bucket_count()." from https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container/unordered_set/insert . See also http://eel.is/c++draft/unord.req#9 . _ So if you return a pointer and not an iterator it is not a problem. But if you want to return an iterator you need to make sure no rehashing happens.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;ab8f9bf180a103e47c1aea6e2338ca716bffc5f9;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;64;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = SymbolTableStorage::const_iterator_;Do you think any of the code using this will need an iterator interface? Or do you really just want the calling code of SymbolTable use it as a pointer to an entry? The only practical difference I see is that the current iterator interface would allow the user to get the next entry. Your original design with the SymbolTableEntry class didn't have a operator++ so I assume this isn't needed. _  _ If so I think this actually should simple be a "const std::string*". _  _ This also would avoid the isSet / isValidIterator issue. Because the pointer would be a nullptr if not available. If I misunderstand something and you do need an iterator, then I suggest to solve the isValidIterator you make this a compat::optional<SymbolTableStorage::const_iterator>.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;ab8f9bf180a103e47c1aea6e2338ca716bffc5f9;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;64;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = SymbolTableStorage::const_iterator_;talking to Mark and Erik again, we agreed that the iterators of the container should not be invalided in any case by inserting a new element. so I guess this should be an unordered_map then instead. _ Question is then if we should go back to the helper class or solve the issue with separating valid and invalid entries somehow else.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;ab8f9bf180a103e47c1aea6e2338ca716bffc5f9;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;64;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = SymbolTableStorage::const_iterator_;I'm sorry if I was not precise with the wording here, and apologize for the confusion I caused (note to self: don't write here when you are sick). _ Marks explanation is correct.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;ab8f9bf180a103e47c1aea6e2338ca716bffc5f9;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;64;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = SymbolTableStorage::const_iterator_;We agreed the requirement that insertion not invalidate existing handles to entries, and that we choose our container and handle type to fulfill that. E.g. const references to things in a std::unordered_map. But a const pointer is equivalent while we don't have a string_view type.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;ab8f9bf180a103e47c1aea6e2338ca716bffc5f9;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;64;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = SymbolTableStorage::const_iterator_;But unordered_map and unordered_set are the same when it comes to what's potential invalidated by insert: pointer/references are not and iterators are. So if we switch from iterator to pointer we can keep it unordered_set. If we don't neither helps.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;ab8f9bf180a103e47c1aea6e2338ca716bffc5f9;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;64;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = SymbolTableStorage::const_iterator_;Why did you agree? Obviously can't help further if I don't know the content of the offline discussion. unordered_map also invalidates (https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container/unordered_map/insert). So this isn't a solution. The insert of the ordered version of both shouldn't invalidate on insert but haven't checked carefully whether there are other pitfalls. _  _ Also what about my suggestion to make this a pointer to the entry (const std::string*) or a view of the entry (compat::string_view)? Why expose the iterator if all you use it for is a pointer (operator * and ->) to the entry? Thus I'm skeptical of using the ordered std::set (or compat::optional) until there is a good reason why a pointer/view isn't sufficient. Given a pointer/view seems to be simplest solution.
gromacs~master~Ibd771444933ef4b01f5a3eca9db3efbbbff2926f;ab8f9bf180a103e47c1aea6e2338ca716bffc5f9;src/gromacs/topology/symtab.h;64;0;0;using SymbolTableEntry = SymbolTableStorage::const_iterator_;Indeed. Paul's wording was ambiguous, and should not have referred to iterators.
