gromacs~release-2020~I2508b6ba4a551a0768b1d5dfd742d97ebec37c7c;a2ff386078533ce810515d88c96d00e3be2babdd;admin/dockerfiles/ci-clang-6/Dockerfile;;0;0;# Set up to only fetch the files and prepare everything for merge requests;I'm unconvinced by this Dockerfile strategy. The contents of ci-basic-dependencies are repeated in every Dockerfile. Maybe that doesn't matter at build time because the docker build engine discovers that they're the same image. But even if true, we only create maintenance problems with the repetition. We should be able to have all the ci-$compiler-$majorversion targets built in the same Dockerfile (which can parallelize once ci-basic-dependencies is done), and it's good that they all share the same base image layer because that limits the disk space required. (That's better than my earlier idea of squashing everything into one image, which minimizes individual image size but maximizes the size of all related images because they can't share the commonalities any more.) So we can still use _  _  docker build $1 --target $1 -t gromacs/continuous-integration:$1 _  docker push gromacs/continuous-integration:$1
