gromacs~master~Ic456d2046f9eb2b86ed8e84741c7f42aee79e717;a06f4b5f5c567b7fdd75bb322f096245d39a94b2;admin/dockerfiles/ci-clang-7/Dockerfile;7;0;0;FROM ubuntu:18.04 as ci-basic-dependencies;Can you clarify whether this intermediate image should be maintained identically to images with the same name in other Dockerfiles?
gromacs~master~Ic456d2046f9eb2b86ed8e84741c7f42aee79e717;a06f4b5f5c567b7fdd75bb322f096245d39a94b2;admin/dockerfiles/ci-clang-7/Dockerfile;7;0;0;FROM ubuntu:18.04 as ci-basic-dependencies;The "as" name is internal to the build. The cache is effective (locally) because all of the Dockerfiles that name a ci-basic-dependencies stage have identical hashes. If it maintainers _should_ update _all_ occurrences of ci-basic-dependencies when they update any one, a helpful reminder would be good, and/or you might consider making a separate tagged image to avoid the redundant maintenance. If this stage is not normative, then consider changing the name or unnaming the stage to avoid giving the false impression that ci-basic-dependencies in one Dockerfile might affect that in another.
gromacs~master~Ic456d2046f9eb2b86ed8e84741c7f42aee79e717;a06f4b5f5c567b7fdd75bb322f096245d39a94b2;admin/dockerfiles/ci-clang-7/Dockerfile;7;0;0;FROM ubuntu:18.04 as ci-basic-dependencies;locally this meant that the image gets reused for the builds, and I was under the impression this is the same for the remote stored images
gromacs~master~Ic456d2046f9eb2b86ed8e84741c7f42aee79e717;a06f4b5f5c567b7fdd75bb322f096245d39a94b2;admin/dockerfiles/ci-docs/Dockerfile;28;0;0;FROM ci-basic-dependencies as ci-clang-7;It doesn't look like ci-basic-dependencies is used anywhere else. Is this just so that you can use the `--target` option to `docker build`?
gromacs~master~Ic456d2046f9eb2b86ed8e84741c7f42aee79e717;a06f4b5f5c567b7fdd75bb322f096245d39a94b2;admin/dockerfiles/ci-docs/Dockerfile;28;0;0;FROM ci-basic-dependencies as ci-clang-7;The stage is reused for other local builds because it produces a layer with the same hash. Local caching is based on layer hashes, which come from tagged images and incremental commands applied to them (and the build "context," I think). Stage names are just for build convenience. Since ci-basic-dependencies and ci-clang-7 are consecutive, they are just internal names for successive layers in the same image. Since your ci-basic-dependencies stages are all identical you _could_ prime the cache by doing a docker build --target ci-basic-dependencies on any one of the Dockerfiles before doing a regular docker build on all of the Dockerfiles, but I'm assuming the docker builds are not happening in parallel on the same host. _  _ I could be mistaken. Naming a stage may allow cache hits that were not possible before, but it is not documented as such. It is possible that named stages are treated differently when pruning the build cache, but I use the default behaviors or prune en masse, so I wouldn't have noticed. _  _ Also, I just checked, and the "as stagename" part does not affect the hash for the "from" layer, so changing the stage name does not invalidate the cache of the layers that follow.
