gromacs~master~Ic456d2046f9eb2b86ed8e84741c7f42aee79e717;30de2e9402ece7b9ee575cf356d6db9a2dae02dc;admin/dockerfiles/ci-docs/Dockerfile;70;0;0;    libomp-dev;https://www.docker.com/blog/intro-guide-to-dockerfile-best-practices/suggests we update and install in the same RUN and also clean out the apt cache when done. or are there later apt stages? _  _ That suggests that we should avoid multiple FROM constructs unless we will use the images so named more than once, because there's a cost to image size and Dockerfile complexity from managing the apt cache clean up (or not). The builder for Doxygen is a notable exception to that principle. Otherwise I think we should be constructing one-shot apt installs so that update, install and clean can be in the same RUN. _  _ lines 3-5 was a local optimization that was good when I was prototyping this locally, but pessimises the production image sizes.
