gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;855eaa5b1772241a3a524bd0484774a3f1bf4065;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1056;1054;1056;    const bool haveHostPmePpComms =;Having revisited this while rebasing, I don't fully understand the intent. Are we aiming to enable GPU update with PP DD or separate PME and *without* direct communication? That's what the above suggests, we are arranging for x D2H for MPI comm from the CPU memory. Am I misunderstanding about the targeted use-case?
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;855eaa5b1772241a3a524bd0484774a3f1bf4065;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1056;1054;1056;            !thisRankHasDuty(cr, DUTY_PME) && !simulationWork.useGpuPmePpCommunication_;Having revisited this while rebasing, I don't fully understand the intent. Are we aiming to enable GPU update with PP DD or separate PME and *without* direct communication? That's what the above suggests, we are arranging for x D2H for MPI comm from the CPU memory. Am I misunderstanding about the targeted use-case?
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;855eaa5b1772241a3a524bd0484774a3f1bf4065;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1056;1054;1056;    const bool haveHostPmePpComms =;I think it would be better if we support the most possible scenarios. We can limit use-cases availability during task assignment. That is my take on this anyways, you have more experience in this.
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;855eaa5b1772241a3a524bd0484774a3f1bf4065;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1056;1054;1056;            !thisRankHasDuty(cr, DUTY_PME) && !simulationWork.useGpuPmePpCommunication_;I think it would be better if we support the most possible scenarios. We can limit use-cases availability during task assignment. That is my take on this anyways, you have more experience in this.
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;f0bf2715b32e28594cafc8a3bea7c5d179018f4f;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1411;0;0;        stateGpu->waitCoordinatesReadyOnHost(AtomLocality::Local)_;Here I can't add a simple assertion on haveCopiedXFromGpu because on search steps we copy outside of do_force() and haveCopiedXFromGpu==false. _  _ At the same time, skipping the wait on search steps does not seem ideal, at least not without the stateGpu internal tracking. _  _ Thoughts?
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;b615d92e31aafcd0a2606c213062dbf3c2e2af41;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1061;0;0;        if (simulationWork.useGpuUpdate && !sendCoordinatesFromGpu);The copy on line 1041 is only made if there is work to do on the CPU (line 1039), which I think is not generally true here. If it is true, then we can prove that by setting a bool after line 1041 that we copied the coordinates, and asserting on it here. Likewise again below.
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;b615d92e31aafcd0a2606c213062dbf3c2e2af41;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1061;0;0;        if (simulationWork.useGpuUpdate && !sendCoordinatesFromGpu);If I understand correctly, you are suggesting that every wait should be preceded by an assertion that a copy has actually happened? _ My plan was that we will have such assertions introduced by If34e37e2bece3ad56afc411e81a9ac1a81ba3613 which is a better way to do the same, if nothing else because it avoid more noise in do_force (and the linked change does this only for x as that's the case where the very simplistic pseudo-blocking copy implementation has serious performance impact in 2020). _  _  _  _ From the pov of this code in this branch, useGpuUpdate should only be true if there is no (PP-PME) communication that needs the coordinates (on the CPU) as this is not supported nor implemented. _  _ However, for a hypothetical case of separate PME rank(s) (without direct GPU copy) supported with GPU update, the code is not correct. copyCoordinatesFromGpu() won't be issued when a separate PME rank that uses a GPU needs to be coordinates sent using staged copy, haveCpuLocalForceWork initialization does not include this case (and it should not). _  _ Given the above, we should never end up here. so I think it is best if an assertion is added like on 1288 which can be removed when this feature gets implemented.
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;b615d92e31aafcd0a2606c213062dbf3c2e2af41;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1061;0;0;        if (simulationWork.useGpuUpdate && !sendCoordinatesFromGpu);> OK thanks. That sounds like a recipe for bugs. _  _ I agree -- that's why I have been suggesting that we introduce a lightweight tracking of the state of CPU buffers wrt GPU and considering the points below I think we should reconsider that. _  _ > And it is not the current behaviour of cudaEventSynchronize - see http://developer.download.nvidia.com/compute/cuda/2_3/toolkit/docs/online/group__CUDART__EVENT_g08241bcf5c5cb686b1882a8492f1e2d9.html.  _  _ Thanks for double-checking me on that, it seems things have either changed over time or I simply had a faulty memory. _  _ Now, the challenge is that we have multiple places where copyCoordinatesFromGpu() is initiated_ as the conditional on 1038 shows at least in the case of stepWork.doNeighborSearch the coordinates are copied in do_md().  _  _  _ > Incidentally this case illustrates why exception-based handling of disappointments is useful. The GpuEventSynchronizer throws something about waiting on an unrecorded event, the StatePropagatorDataGpu adds info about x on host, and here we add something about expecting them available on the host after an update. _  _ Thanks. Use-case noted.
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;b615d92e31aafcd0a2606c213062dbf3c2e2af41;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1061;0;0;        if (simulationWork.useGpuUpdate && !sendCoordinatesFromGpu);OK thanks. That sounds like a recipe for bugs. And it is not the current behaviour of cudaEventSynchronize - see http://developer.download.nvidia.com/compute/cuda/2_3/toolkit/docs/online/group__CUDART__EVENT_g08241bcf5c5cb686b1882a8492f1e2d9.html. We're just ignoring the errors except in debug builds. So in debug builds these will assert when we wait on a thing that wasn't set up with cudaEventRecord. The assertions that I proposed mean the diagnostic message is useful. _  _ Incidentally this case illustrates why exception-based handling of disappointments is useful. The GpuEventSynchronizer throws something about waiting on an unrecorded event, the StatePropagatorDataGpu adds info about x on host, and here we add something about expecting them available on the host after an update.
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;b615d92e31aafcd0a2606c213062dbf3c2e2af41;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1061;0;0;        if (simulationWork.useGpuUpdate && !sendCoordinatesFromGpu);Sure, something should document unreachable code, and an assertion is good for that. _  _ Yes, I think that an assertion before a wait that would hang is a better user and developer experience because the message leads the developer to the problem without having to be able to reproduce. Same as an assertion against nullptr that would otherwise be about to segfault.
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;b615d92e31aafcd0a2606c213062dbf3c2e2af41;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1061;0;0;        if (simulationWork.useGpuUpdate && !sendCoordinatesFromGpu);> Sure, something should document unreachable code, and an assertion is good for that. _ >  _ > Yes, I think that an assertion before a wait that would hang is a better user and developer experience because the message leads the developer to the problem without having to be able to reproduce. Same as an assertion against nullptr that would otherwise be about to segfault. _  _ Note that these waits, even if due to a bug or mistake during refactoring would end up issued when unnecessary, will _not_ hang! _ cudaEventSynchronize(), when called on an event that has not been triggered (at all) or one that has already been waited on (i.e. "stale"), will simply return successfully. _  _ Therefore adding an assertion would only serve the purpose of documenting / ensuring that code paths / combinations of features only get executed if intended to work and are supported.
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;b615d92e31aafcd0a2606c213062dbf3c2e2af41;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1064;0;0;            stateGpu->waitCoordinatesReadyOnHost(AtomLocality::Local)_;Do we agree that this should be converted to a: _  _   GMX_RELEASE_ASSERT(false, "GPU update and separate PME ranks are only supported with GPU direct communication!")
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;b615d92e31aafcd0a2606c213062dbf3c2e2af41;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1064;0;0;            stateGpu->waitCoordinatesReadyOnHost(AtomLocality::Local)_;> I would say that DD case with GPU update is equally untested with and without direct comms. Also, one can enable either of these cases only using environment variable. _  _ I am not referring to the DD+GPU update path (and that is probably even less tested without direct GPU comm), but to the separate PME case, in particular with 1 PP. Is that supported or not -- there are conflicting hints in the code about it.
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;b615d92e31aafcd0a2606c213062dbf3c2e2af41;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1064;0;0;            stateGpu->waitCoordinatesReadyOnHost(AtomLocality::Local)_;It should work even without direct communications. But who wants it in this case?
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;b615d92e31aafcd0a2606c213062dbf3c2e2af41;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1064;0;0;            stateGpu->waitCoordinatesReadyOnHost(AtomLocality::Local)_;Just tested 1 PP + 1 PME  and that case is still behind an assertion -- and until recently the copyCoordinatesFromGpu() did not consider this case either. _ However, recent code in I57e3ad3b8a571ec244989e888afd5cfcbaf9b75e seems to be catering for this case (i.e. haveHostPmePpComms). _  _ Having some part of the code declare a feature unsupported, while some other add last-minute changes that assumes otherwise is confusing. In particular (but not only) to be able to reason about correctness of release code, we'd really benefit from consistent assumptions across the code. _  _ I am leaning towards adding the suggestion assertion. I would however like us to resolve the inconsistencies/lack of clarity with assumption around haveHostPmePpComms
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;b615d92e31aafcd0a2606c213062dbf3c2e2af41;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1064;0;0;            stateGpu->waitCoordinatesReadyOnHost(AtomLocality::Local)_;I would say that DD case with GPU update is equally untested with and without direct comms. Also, one can enable either of these cases only using environment variable.
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;b615d92e31aafcd0a2606c213062dbf3c2e2af41;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1064;0;0;            stateGpu->waitCoordinatesReadyOnHost(AtomLocality::Local)_;Yes, any unwanted or untested code paths should be blocked. This is in general, and especially for the new GPU code, a largest source of bugs. It will lead to bugs that are difficult to identify and in worst case "slightly" incorrect results due to missing synchronization. An unwanted assertion failure identifies itself and is easy to fix.
gromacs~release-2020~Ia6641147bbec1186b54c1445d36dc31000eae9c4;b615d92e31aafcd0a2606c213062dbf3c2e2af41;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1064;0;0;            stateGpu->waitCoordinatesReadyOnHost(AtomLocality::Local)_;Do you mean that it's been tested and it is a known to be working use-case? _  _ > But who wants it in this case? _  _ The question is what is implemented and tested. If something is not well-tested it risks being a source of bugs (and we should strongly consider to prevent such code-paths from being used). _  _ A user can easily end up triggering this code path not intentionally but simply because their setup ends up on this code-path_ e.g. because they only have an MPI version, or because direct communication init fails.
