gromacs~master~I0c0129fd881c84d366c1745ee3d9e3a8caf8633e;b7fcc3c9c68dd4e4f99e449f887031f2bc6a3ed6;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;171;0;0;    {;The problem is that the whole ListOfLists class gets instantiated including all members. So the member needs to compile. When using a non-arithmetic type, the first of your suggestions produces: _ error: ISO C++ forbids declaration of ‘appendListOfLists’ with no type [-fpermissive] _      appendListOfLists(const ListOfLists<U>& listOfLists, const T offset) _  _ Your second suggestion produces a much longer error output.
gromacs~master~I0c0129fd881c84d366c1745ee3d9e3a8caf8633e;b7fcc3c9c68dd4e4f99e449f887031f2bc6a3ed6;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;171;0;0;    {;The int in "typename std::enable_if_t<std::is_same<T, U>::value, int> = 0" is just sugar that means that at the point of call the only thing the compiler has to deduce is T and U, and then to evaluate is_same. It bears no relationship to the offset computation (which uses operands of type T or U), as can be seen from the fact that the second example also works.
gromacs~master~I0c0129fd881c84d366c1745ee3d9e3a8caf8633e;b7fcc3c9c68dd4e4f99e449f887031f2bc6a3ed6;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;171;0;0;    {;Either _  _     template<typename U, _              typename std::enable_if_t<std::is_same<T, U>::value, int> = 0> _     void _     appendListOfLists(const ListOfLists<U>& listOfLists, const T offset) _  _ or simply _  _     void _     appendListOfLists(const ListOfLists<T>& listOfLists, const T offset) _  _ because what is simpler than using the the same type if they have to be the same?
gromacs~master~I0c0129fd881c84d366c1745ee3d9e3a8caf8633e;b7fcc3c9c68dd4e4f99e449f887031f2bc6a3ed6;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;171;0;0;    {;The compiler has to find a U that matches T in order to find the type of the second template parameter (here, int), which has a default value of 0 (but could be anything) because it is is neither named nor used thereafter.
