gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;85;0;0;    void emplaceBack(ArrayRef<const T> values);this is much closer to std::vector::push_back than emplace_back
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;85;0;0;    void emplaceBack(ArrayRef<const T> values);We can argue about this. We are passing in a view and creating a list, so from that perspective it's closer to emplaceBack. But I am not a C++ expert.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;85;0;0;    void emplaceBack(ArrayRef<const T> values);The relevant distinction is about when the constructor of the new object is called. emplace_back exists to facilitate users writing code that does not need to either create a named object of the type, or explicitly call the constructor of the type, because v.push_back(RedundantNameOfTheType(the,args)) is useless verbosity for both reader and compiler, and not needing to do it creates some optimization opportunities where the compiler does not need to generate both a constructor call and a copy operation (for push_back). _  _ I now do see Berk's point, that the argument to emplaceBack is the argument to construct the newly constructed notional list, so I'm happy with emplace over push in the name.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;85;0;0;    void emplaceBack(ArrayRef<const T> values);A little closer, but without defining the member type, I think there is some wiggle room, and it doesn't make sense to have pushBack() without arguments. `pushBack({})` or `pushBack(someType())` would be preferable, though, if acceptable.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;85;0;0;    void emplaceBack(ArrayRef<const T> values);> the argument to emplaceBack is the argument to construct the newly constructed notional list _  _ Exactly. The parameter is not clearly of ListOfLists::value_type, but arguably usable as the argument to ListOfLists::value_type(...), if ListOfLists::value_type existed as more than a notion.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;92;0;0;    void emplaceBack() { listRanges_.push_back(listRanges_.back())_ };alternatively you could just let people emplaceBack({}), which makes very clear at the point of call that an empty vector is appended, and we anyway should cover this in the unit tests because a named ArrayRef parameter could refer to an empty view
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;92;0;0;    void emplaceBack() { listRanges_.push_back(listRanges_.back())_ };Yes, vector.tcc is included by vector.h, so the code can be inlined.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;92;0;0;    void emplaceBack() { listRanges_.push_back(listRanges_.back())_ };@Eric As written, emplaceBack(ArrayRef<T> values) must construct an ArrayRef from whatever is passed, so the ArrayRef() constructor is called (so its begin and end are nullptr). If insert() is inlined (and IIRC it is not), then the compiler will see that values.begin() and values.end() are the same nullptr value, so the code in insert() gets optimized out. _  _ So having the extra member is likely to be an optimization in the cases where the caller knows they are appending an empty view, and thus could call either emplaceBack({}) or the proposed emplaceBack(). If so, I'd leave it out of the initial class design, and add it when we can see some callers that are using it in places that we care about. At that point, it becomes reasonable to have observed the performance effect. _  _ There's probably a reason that std::vector doesn't have a no-arg flavor of push_back(). That might relate to the ability to call code like _  _  auto theSize = v.size()_ _  v.resize(theSize+n)_ _  _ in the special case of n==1, to get a default-inserted element.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;92;0;0;    void emplaceBack() { listRanges_.push_back(listRanges_.back())_ };One potential solution to this is to remove the code that automatically includes the self-exclusions and always push in at least one exclusion. This may have some small performance benefit as that conditional is currently checked on every domain each step.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;92;0;0;    void emplaceBack() { listRanges_.push_back(listRanges_.back())_ };std::vector<T>::push_back() would require that T is default constructable, which is not a condition of std::vector, and would be redundant with push_back(value_type()), which isn't the sort of extra feature you normally see in std::
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;92;0;0;    void emplaceBack() { listRanges_.push_back(listRanges_.back())_ };Correction: the conventional named requirement would be DefaultInsertable, which is a requirement for std::vector<T>::resize(size_type) but not for any of the std::vector<T>::push_back overloads.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;92;0;0;    void emplaceBack() { listRanges_.push_back(listRanges_.back())_ };I am now leaning to pushBack() as emplaceBack() implies that you pass constructor arguments, but there is no constructor defined here. _  _ If there are only self exclusions, the DD topology generation code only calls emplaceBack({}). In gcc the insert implementation is in vector.tcc, which is a header file, but I didn't figure out if this gets, indirectly, included by vector.h
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;92;0;0;    void emplaceBack() { listRanges_.push_back(listRanges_.back())_ };I think that would be preferable and I believe it was my original suggestion. Berk expressed concern over performance impact. Will ArrayRef implicitly initialize from a braced list or would an additional overload be necessary for std::initialize_list<int>? If the ArrayRef signature will not catch a braced init list, then I don't think empty-list optimization would be possible, since a std::initializer_list<int> overload would match before a parameter type that accepts an empty aggregate initialization.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;5c7459b5df49939afa0dbe0c67d49d5dbeb79855;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;92;0;0;    void emplaceBack() { listRanges_.push_back(listRanges_.back())_ };Changed to pushBack(). _  _ Since insert() seems to be inlined, there is no issue. _ I also thought about removing the exclusion list entirely when there are only self exclusions. This just needs a single conditional in the pair list module. But then some extra code is needed for the gmx_localtop_t to moltype conversion I just added in convert_tpr.cpp (or we should get rid of the tpr reduction).
