gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;4f0312759436e1a160464287eb77a34cb586e90e;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;155;151;155;template<typename T>;Okay, I understand - I was only looking at this interface and not the use in the code. _  _ From looking at some of the usage, a constructor that consumes two vectors might be better then. _  _ Looking at this code for example,  _ https://gerrit.gromacs.org/c/gromacs/+/14739/1/src/gromacs/mdlib/constr.cpp _ it would be great to have a builder class that itself holds the two vectors that you define in the function. Then, at the function return you would call listOfListsBuilder.build() that calls the constructor of the ListOfLists with the two vectors that it held (and potentially does a consistency check on the vectors, if we want to avoid having that in the constructor for performance reasons in other parts of the code) _  _ Then, this also would not need to be a friend_ listRanges() and elements() can become member functions again - they might be called listRangesView(), if we want to keep the notion that it is not the underlying vector, but a view to it that is returned. _  _ Sorry for the back-and-forth, I thought there was a way to hide the serialization work in an extra class, since that does not work, I would rather stay away from using a friend class here.
