gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;9d1dae5e79fbc16627556af162e769aaf19d4774;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;65;64;65; * \note This class could be templated on element type to support other data;Done
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;9d1dae5e79fbc16627556af162e769aaf19d4774;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;65;64;65; * \note This class could be templated on element type to support other data;Currently I didn't see any, so I did not template it yet.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;e9d73b7b934048c8612861135f1755bf2dd087c7;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;164;163;164;    //! The ranges of the lists, list i uses range \p listRanges_[i], \p listRanges_[i+1].;This setup comes with size + 1, size - 1 math that's prone to off by 1 errors. I wonder if it would be much of a performance hit to describe this as either two vectors or a vector of pairs of (start_index, num_elements), that would make the code clearer. _  _ I guess whether that would make an impact would depend on the ratio of size of the elements to number of lists.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;e9d73b7b934048c8612861135f1755bf2dd087c7;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;164;163;164;    //! The ranges of the lists, list i uses range \p listRanges_[i], \p listRanges_[i+1].;Agree that that is bug-prone. That is one of the disadvantages of t_blocka. But here this is fully hidden from the outside, well managed and tested. Using pairs causes overhead and is more complicated in most places here.
