gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;b578e5f30507270b1f4bd6db9179ae2f222460eb;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;75;0;0;    ListOfLists() {};if this does not throw it should be marked noexcept
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;b578e5f30507270b1f4bd6db9179ae2f222460eb;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;75;0;0;    ListOfLists() {};I think it can throw, because we create a std::vector with one element which is not guaranteed to not throw.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;b578e5f30507270b1f4bd6db9179ae2f222460eb;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;75;0;0;    ListOfLists() {};The default constructor is implicitly non-throwing based on inspection by the compiler, but this should probably be explitly `default`ed: _     ListOfLists() = default_
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;b578e5f30507270b1f4bd6db9179ae2f222460eb;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;75;0;0;    ListOfLists() {};Right. It is implementation dependent, and the compiler knows better than we do, so we cannot document it, unless we want to say that its exception status depends on that of std::vector, but that seems unnecessary. If it is important to client code, the client code can use `std::is_nothrow_constructible`.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;b578e5f30507270b1f4bd6db9179ae2f222460eb;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;75;0;0;    ListOfLists() {};But what the compiler says might depend on implementation. So we can't document if it might throw or not. _  _ Forgot about the =default, will fix.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;b578e5f30507270b1f4bd6db9179ae2f222460eb;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;75;0;0;    ListOfLists() {};My point is that we are supposed to rely on the compiler for this. A trivial default constructor should be defined with `ListOfLists() = default`, and the compiler will automatically mark it noexcept if possible, and not noexcept if not possible, based on the members. See, for instance, https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/default_constructor _  _ The default constructor is one of six special member functions for which it is the compiler's job to determine the exception specification. I think the others are the other "default"able or implicitly definable ones, possibly excepting the move constructor and assignment operator, but I can't recall right now. _  _ Anyway, leaving off "noexcept" is the right thing to do here, but you should use `= default_` instead of `{}`.
gromacs~master~Ib4b7f5f0e57b82c939f53e9805dc16e9d76db22b;b578e5f30507270b1f4bd6db9179ae2f222460eb;src/gromacs/utility/listoflists.h;75;0;0;    ListOfLists() {};But we can't rely on the compiler. The stdlib specs should tell that and it does say non-throwing for constructing a std::vector with one (or more) elements.
