gromacs~release-2020~I57e3ad3b8a571ec244989e888afd5cfcbaf9b75e;0500651d98f73d8c888338281daa9de4c683d048;src/gromacs/taskassignment/decidegpuusage.cpp;513;0;0;        && gmx_mtop_interaction_count(mtop, IF_CONSTRAINT) > 0);I was pre-empting the discussion that when not using DD and if the constraint algorithm is not SHAKE, then the code generated for PS7 will never call the mtop methods. PS9 will call them, unless the compiler has enough information to permit it to re-order the calls to the point when PS7 calls them. To do that, it would either need to be able to inline the function, or know that the functions are pure (have no side effects) otherwise e.g. the std::string constructor of errorMessage could be modifying the memory of mtop, etc. The mtop functions aren't pure, so the compiler will not do that, so technically PS9 is a pessimization. But clarity is much much more important here than performance. (We call these methods in a few places during setup, so caching the result in something related to mtop makes sense, but that's a job for another time).
gromacs~release-2020~I57e3ad3b8a571ec244989e888afd5cfcbaf9b75e;0500651d98f73d8c888338281daa9de4c683d048;src/gromacs/taskassignment/decidegpuusage.cpp;513;0;0;        && gmx_mtop_interaction_count(mtop, IF_CONSTRAINT) > 0);Sorry, I confused IF_CONSTRAINT with the logic in gmx_mtop_ftype_count(mtop, F_CONSTR). _  _ There's useful clarity for humans in having _  _  const bool hasConstrainedBonds = gmx_mtop_ftype_count(mtop, F_CONSTR) > 0_ _  const bool hasAnyConstraints = hasConstrainedBonds || gmx_mtop_interaction_count(mtop, IF_CONSTRAINT) > 0_ _  _ and let the compiler see that we only need to compute these in special cases, so it can rearrange the assembly code to avoid unnecessary function calls.
