gromacs~release-2020~Ib41e012b9f56591c80046b44891dd77af5a2f938;d359c2f6979c9c64033c5d154b88d769ddbe5b15;src/gromacs/taskassignment/taskassignment.cpp;357;0;0;        gmx_fatal(FARGS,;Conceivable, but many more conditions could produce them (e.g. bad_alloc anywhere, exception in one sim of multi-sim set, or user gpu ids make sense on some nodes and not others). However those will generally also occur on a node's master rank, so the above hack is useful in most cases. _  _ This is the same problem we've always had (fatal error in such cases were also poorly reported) and has the same general solution (communicate to understand there's an error to report, reduce them to a reporting rank, understand what duplicates exist and should be reported or not, and report them together with annotation about which rank generated them). _  _ Agree about a comment that an exception thrown on a rank that isn't the master of a physical node is being handled here.
gromacs~release-2020~Ib41e012b9f56591c80046b44891dd77af5a2f938;d359c2f6979c9c64033c5d154b88d769ddbe5b15;src/gromacs/taskassignment/taskassignment.cpp;357;0;0;        gmx_fatal(FARGS,;Only ranks that are 0 within their physical node can have already exited. Otherwise, every rank proceeds through the barriers and we reach here when some other rank had an exception. (Maybe we should now throw all of them like line 343 here? We've made the attempt not to spam the user with the same message from every rank)
gromacs~release-2020~Ib41e012b9f56591c80046b44891dd77af5a2f938;d359c2f6979c9c64033c5d154b88d769ddbe5b15;src/gromacs/taskassignment/taskassignment.cpp;357;0;0;        gmx_fatal(FARGS,;Done
