gromacs~release-2020~Ib2f2861702e111f67c38b0c9d65ccbe4c81a0ccd;4fdaa807106a12c767a1dac4068251b66ef161af;cmake/FindHWLOC.cmake;274;0;0;    include(CheckFunctionExists);check for a v2 function here, as appropriate. see below. it is better to fail to find the library, than to find one and then have to check it and when it fails the user is still left with useless CMake cache variables, so they end up deleting the whole thing
gromacs~release-2020~Ib2f2861702e111f67c38b0c9d65ccbe4c81a0ccd;4fdaa807106a12c767a1dac4068251b66ef161af;cmake/FindHWLOC.cmake;274;0;0;    include(CheckFunctionExists);ok but that means gmx_test_hwloc is not very useful in handling the problem at hand, because it doesn't necessarily run at compilation time _  _ the issue can be handled well by using the kind of logic in the top of the new test source file at CMake time, storing the expected hwloc version in a config.h define, and computing it again at compile time. #if they don't match then give a compile time #error. _  _ now a clear message can be given that the headers found now differ in version (we report both) from those found at CMake time, so the user should fix the environment (eg load the right module) or remove the hwloc cache entries and run CMake again, or delete everything and run CMake again. _  _ the current run time error message is better than a crash but it still leads to a question on gmx-users in many cases, as here
gromacs~release-2020~Ib2f2861702e111f67c38b0c9d65ccbe4c81a0ccd;4fdaa807106a12c767a1dac4068251b66ef161af;cmake/FindHWLOC.cmake;274;0;0;    include(CheckFunctionExists);users might configure in one shell and make in another and that's a significant way to have mismatched module/environment state. Devs do that all the time. One can't detect such a mismatch until at least make time. _  _ your approach does indeed help when a user does CMake, changes the modules and does CMake again, but the price of doing so is a compilation and run every time CMake is called. that doesn't scale to every possible case of environment change. and does not cover the case in my first paragraph.
gromacs~release-2020~Ib2f2861702e111f67c38b0c9d65ccbe4c81a0ccd;4fdaa807106a12c767a1dac4068251b66ef161af;cmake/FindHWLOC.cmake;274;0;0;    include(CheckFunctionExists);the check you suggested last is probably going to look like the one I previously suggested
gromacs~release-2020~Ib2f2861702e111f67c38b0c9d65ccbe4c81a0ccd;4fdaa807106a12c767a1dac4068251b66ef161af;cmake/FindHWLOC.cmake;274;0;0;    include(CheckFunctionExists);dumb question, but why do you think it is better to check this during compile time instead of configure time? _ Right now this checks every time something hwloc related changes during configuration and bails out before the user has to go through compilation, and the way the test function works it only uses include directories that are also used during the actual compilation later (so there are no surprises due to that). _ I can improve the output of the test function to properly state what mismatch was detected, and also include a static assert or preprocessor check in the hardtopology.cpp source file
gromacs~release-2020~Ib2f2861702e111f67c38b0c9d65ccbe4c81a0ccd;4fdaa807106a12c767a1dac4068251b66ef161af;cmake/FindHWLOC.cmake;274;0;0;    include(CheckFunctionExists);The issue is that it can find the correct library, with linking working as well because it links against the correct version of the library, but during actual compilation the wrong headers are included. I don't see a way to use the check_function_exists cmake function to make sure that the correct header is included, or to check the version information found in the header
gromacs~release-2020~Ib2f2861702e111f67c38b0c9d65ccbe4c81a0ccd;4fdaa807106a12c767a1dac4068251b66ef161af;cmake/FindHWLOC.cmake;274;0;0;    include(CheckFunctionExists);so, I think I came up with a solution now that doesn't need another test file or compiling/running of a test binary. _ I also added a preprocessor check in the hardwaretopology.cpp file if users are creative with their build process :)
