gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;50ab2e480d2e609d67d1c0fc4c08d096356f25b7;.clang-format;11;0;0;AllowShortBlocksOnASingleLine: false;I personally would like all the AllowShort* to be true
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;50ab2e480d2e609d67d1c0fc4c08d096356f25b7;.clang-format;11;0;0;AllowShortBlocksOnASingleLine: false;Switching these four `false` values to `true` increases the diff size substantially. More granular tweaking might produce output closer to our current style. If tweaks are necessary to update to prettier style, I think that should go in follow-up changes.
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;50ab2e480d2e609d67d1c0fc4c08d096356f25b7;.clang-format;11;0;0;AllowShortBlocksOnASingleLine: false;I'm ambivalent, but would lean towards whichever is less disruptive to the current formatting. Do you have a sense of this?
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;50ab2e480d2e609d67d1c0fc4c08d096356f25b7;.clang-format;40;0;0;ColumnLimit:     0;I have much better-looking results when I set this to '80'. It is not a hard limit and, in conjunction with the Penalty options, does a pretty good job of breaking lines around operators
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;50ab2e480d2e609d67d1c0fc4c08d096356f25b7;.clang-format;40;0;0;ColumnLimit:     0;I responded to the comment thread in the older patch set.
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;50ab2e480d2e609d67d1c0fc4c08d096356f25b7;.clang-format;40;0;0;ColumnLimit:     0;I agree we should have non-zero. There was previous discussion on this on patch-set 6. In case you can't see it because it was private previously a screenshot of it: https://imgur.com/a/aZiNmgQ
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;53599a13c8933b9819a0ce5cb1412107d68f87cf;.clang-format;40;0;0;ColumnLimit:     0;I would strong prefer if we use some non-zero value. E.g. 120.
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;53599a13c8933b9819a0ce5cb1412107d68f87cf;.clang-format;40;0;0;ColumnLimit:     0;The competition between the line length penalty and other rules doesn't seem to be well-documented, but the AllowShort... options produce some >80 character lines. Most of the broken lines I see look like lines that were more than 80 characters with no competing rules.
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;53599a13c8933b9819a0ce5cb1412107d68f87cf;.clang-format;40;0;0;ColumnLimit:     0;Yes, using 80 as a soft limit produces nice looking code and probably also minimal changes, as most code used 80 as a target.
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;53599a13c8933b9819a0ce5cb1412107d68f87cf;.clang-format;40;0;0;ColumnLimit:     0;I agree it's more convenient to not have to worry about this. But we should carefully consider what length we want. 120 sounds reasonable though.
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;53599a13c8933b9819a0ce5cb1412107d68f87cf;.clang-format;40;0;0;ColumnLimit:     0;IIRC, that will make clang-format to remove line breaks if any line has been wrapped shorter than that. I.e., it is not just a maximum length, but rather a target length for all lines.
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;53599a13c8933b9819a0ce5cb1412107d68f87cf;.clang-format;40;0;0;ColumnLimit:     0;What causes the wrapping of many lines with the current settings then?
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;53599a13c8933b9819a0ce5cb1412107d68f87cf;.clang-format;40;0;0;ColumnLimit:     0;My observation is that setting it to 0 is equivalent to setting to infinity, which means line breaks are removed in many cases. The penalty for long lines is adjustable, so setting a shorter target length, such as 80, works great, and I don't see any down side to erring on the side of shorter. I don't know if there is a separate configurable to set a hard limit on line length, but this one seems to be more of a target line length, as noted by Teemu. 0 produces unacceptably long lines, and 120 produces too many lines that are more than 120 characters. 80 produces formatting that looks good to me, in a cursory review.
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;53599a13c8933b9819a0ce5cb1412107d68f87cf;.clang-format;40;0;0;ColumnLimit:     0;Indeed. That's why the previous compromise was 0. But I think we have way too much code with unreasonable long lines. So I think we should reconsider this. I think the downside of it merging lines is much smaller than the upside of really having a maximum and not having to remind people in code review to honor the maximum.
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;53599a13c8933b9819a0ce5cb1412107d68f87cf;.clang-format;40;0;0;ColumnLimit:     0;Equally happy with 100 or 110. Only said 120 because we agreed on that for maximum previously. I think anything >80 and <=120 would be compatible with the previous discussion.
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;53599a13c8933b9819a0ce5cb1412107d68f87cf;.clang-format;77;0;0;PointerAlignment: Right;Clang-format insists on doing alignment on the right side. If we want to switch to clang-format the options we have are: _ a) Right aligned pointer, as the child patch shows. There is always one space to the left and only space on the right if alignment is needed. _ b) Middle aligned pointer. Meaning there is at least one space on both sides. Alignment spaces are added on the right. I personally don't like this because this causes e.g. "void f(int * a, int * b)_" _ c) Left aligned pointer. There is never space on the left. Alignment is on the right. Disadvantages of this is that it changes our current style everywhere and that it is inconsistent if more than one pointer is declared on one line. Because in that case clang-format insists on right aligned. Example: _  _     int* c_ _     int *d, *e_ _  _ d) Don't align. I personally think this makes large declarations less readable.
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;53599a13c8933b9819a0ce5cb1412107d68f87cf;admin/uncrustify.cfg;228;0;0;sp_arith                                 = add      # ignore/add/remove/force;Here there doesn't seem to be an option to treat * and / different from the other operators, so then I would prefer ignore.
gromacs~master~Ia1880f5670714c497f683be10a9e72b505ca6c5a;53599a13c8933b9819a0ce5cb1412107d68f87cf;admin/uncrustify.cfg;228;0;0;sp_arith                                 = add      # ignore/add/remove/force;Yes ignore is the only option to keep your preferred style. That's why we set it to ignore originally. The problem with that is: First ignore means that we are still manually responsible to correctly do the whitespace. This has two problems: a) not everyone follows the same style causing inconsistencies b) it requires manual effort c) it makes automatic code transformation impossible without ending up with less well formatted code (https://gerrit.gromacs.org/#/c/6289/7/share/template/template.cpp@51 is caused ignore rule). _ Second clang-format doesn't have a ignore option (on purpose for the above reasons). Thus if we want to switch to clang-format we don't have an option. _  _ The question is whether the advantages of clang-format (proper parser) and the ones listed above are sufficient for you to be OK with having a non-preferred style.
