gromacs~master~Ia48d8eb63e38d0d23eefd755dcc228ff9b66d3e6;92029536e40f7948bb9a614e5bd61d3446ee5911;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_spread.cu;213;211;213;    atomX.y    =  kernelParams.atoms.d_coordinates[ atomIndexGlobal*DIM + YY]_;This vectorizes ans saves one load instruction. Also, I am not sure removing staging is beneficial, is it?
gromacs~master~Ia48d8eb63e38d0d23eefd755dcc228ff9b66d3e6;92029536e40f7948bb9a614e5bd61d3446ee5911;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_spread.cu;213;211;213;    atomX.x    =  kernelParams.atoms.d_coordinates[ atomIndexGlobal*DIM + XX]_;The load into shared memory was latency limited in the cases we looked at and probably will be worse with future hardware. Even at full occupancy there are not enough instructions to hide the latency of the load from global and store to shared.  _  _ 4 threads per atom helps here as well. It allows more loads to be active at any one time (so the effective occupancy is higher as each thread is doing more work or more accurately there are less idle threads). _  _ Improving this bottleneck is the key reason for the improvements on larger sizes.
gromacs~master~Ia48d8eb63e38d0d23eefd755dcc228ff9b66d3e6;92029536e40f7948bb9a614e5bd61d3446ee5911;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_spread.cu;213;211;213;    atomX.y    =  kernelParams.atoms.d_coordinates[ atomIndexGlobal*DIM + YY]_;The load into shared memory was latency limited in the cases we looked at and probably will be worse with future hardware. Even at full occupancy there are not enough instructions to hide the latency of the load from global and store to shared.  _  _ 4 threads per atom helps here as well. It allows more loads to be active at any one time (so the effective occupancy is higher as each thread is doing more work or more accurately there are less idle threads). _  _ Improving this bottleneck is the key reason for the improvements on larger sizes.
gromacs~master~Ia48d8eb63e38d0d23eefd755dcc228ff9b66d3e6;92029536e40f7948bb9a614e5bd61d3446ee5911;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_spread.cu;213;211;213;    atomX.x    =  kernelParams.atoms.d_coordinates[ atomIndexGlobal*DIM + XX]_;> The load into shared memory was latency limited in the cases we looked at and probably will be worse with future hardware. Even at full occupancy there are not enough instructions to hide the latency of the load from global and store to shared.  _  _ Do you have performance data that supports that? I struggle to imagine that nicely coalesced loads into shared memory are outperformed by the above non-coalescing four consecutive 4-byte loads. If anything, this implementation will be instruction latency limited as it issues far more global loads than necessary. _  _ > 4 threads per atom helps here as well. It allows more loads to be active at any one time (so the effective occupancy is higher as each thread is doing more work or more accurately there are less idle threads). _  _ The benchmarks seem to show that 4 threads/atom has limited use (a few % on V100), so that does not seem to be a good reason to remove the manual prefetching.
