gromacs~master~I66233f1c790fc5092fb1babaed2ec3ebf16416de;03ab56d1d03ef07a915ad58b3a3b67f47096ee9b;src/gromacs/mdtypes/simulation_workload.h;137;0;0;        //! If we have calculation of short range nonbondeds on CPU;Does the include free-energy non-bondeds or not?
gromacs~master~I66233f1c790fc5092fb1babaed2ec3ebf16416de;03ab56d1d03ef07a915ad58b3a3b67f47096ee9b;src/gromacs/mdtypes/simulation_workload.h;137;0;0;        //! If we have calculation of short range nonbondeds on CPU;Good point, it does not. We should have flags corresponding to that too (as well as domainLifetimeWorkload flags. Will update docs for now, I want to minimize  changes.
gromacs~master~I66233f1c790fc5092fb1babaed2ec3ebf16416de;03ab56d1d03ef07a915ad58b3a3b67f47096ee9b;src/gromacs/taskassignment/decidesimulationworkload.cpp;66;0;0;    simulationWorkload.useGpuPmeFft              = (pmeRunMode == PmeRunMode::Mixed)_;There are several use cases that these should be constructed to fit: _  _  * organizing data transfers to satisfy input requirements _  * calling buffer ops and/or work kernels and enqueuing related sync points _  * organizing data transfers to consume the output _  _ The PmeRunModes have granularity that suits this, as it is clear where positions are needed, where kernels will run, and where forces are produced. _  _ useGpuPmeFft is useful for some of the cases for the latter stages of PME work, but we'd have to use logic like useCpuPme || useGpuPmeFft to understand whether the CPU has PME forces to contribute. But useGpuPme isn't useful for understanding whether the GPU has PME forces to contribute, and useGpuPme && !useGpuPmeFft is what we'd have to use. _  _ That suggests we just use a PmeRunMode in simulationWorkload and accept that sometimes we'll || two of them together for understanding things like transferring positions and forces for subsequent work. That will need some adjustments in do_md and do_force. That's work, but probably helps preventing bugs, as the current bools are already not directly useful for understanding where forces are produced. _  _ Or that we replace use*Pme* with e.g. pmeNeedsPositionsOnCpu, pmeNeedsPositionsOnGpu, pmeProducesForcesOnCpu, pmeProducesForcesOnGpu. I don't think that's a good idea, because only four of those sixteen possibilities are actually implemented (ie. the ones expressed by PmeRunMode) so we'd have a bunch of things to assert.
gromacs~master~I66233f1c790fc5092fb1babaed2ec3ebf16416de;03ab56d1d03ef07a915ad58b3a3b67f47096ee9b;src/gromacs/taskassignment/decidesimulationworkload.cpp;66;0;0;    simulationWorkload.useGpuPmeFft              = (pmeRunMode == PmeRunMode::Mixed)_;useGpuPmeFft is in fact unused, so it can be removed. We might end up needing something in a workload data structure that describes whether the CPU or GPU does FFT work, but right now that is internal to PME, so I'll remove the flag. _  _ I think PmeRunMode is not the type of data that is a good fit to be exposed through workload data structures. I could store the PmeRunMode and have a getter do the || every time simulationWork.useGpuPme() is called. I am not too fond of having to do a logical or for the a condition that we'll use many times across the code, but the main reason why I prefer to leave things as they are is that I'd rather not have a getter for only one of the flags and not for the others. I've planned to move to getters for all flags exactly so things can be combined without storing everything at initialization as well as because that would allows changing the storage transparently. _  _ The latter option I am not fond of either.
gromacs~master~I66233f1c790fc5092fb1babaed2ec3ebf16416de;03ab56d1d03ef07a915ad58b3a3b67f47096ee9b;src/gromacs/taskassignment/decidesimulationworkload.cpp;66;0;0;    simulationWorkload.useGpuPmeFft              = (pmeRunMode == PmeRunMode::Mixed)_;I agree that having proper getters (in the form of accessing directly what kind of operation is requested and then returning the correct decision whether to perform the action on GPU or CPU) will be nice, but this is clean up that I think should be now done in a follow-up.
gromacs~master~I66233f1c790fc5092fb1babaed2ec3ebf16416de;03ab56d1d03ef07a915ad58b3a3b67f47096ee9b;src/gromacs/taskassignment/decidesimulationworkload.cpp;66;0;0;    simulationWorkload.useGpuPmeFft              = (pmeRunMode == PmeRunMode::Mixed)_;I wouldn't worry about an ||, that shouldn't be more than a cycle.
gromacs~master~I66233f1c790fc5092fb1babaed2ec3ebf16416de;03ab56d1d03ef07a915ad58b3a3b67f47096ee9b;src/gromacs/taskassignment/decidesimulationworkload.cpp;66;0;0;    simulationWorkload.useGpuPmeFft              = (pmeRunMode == PmeRunMode::Mixed)_;We've already demonstrated why useGpuPme is not easy to use/understand (https://gerrit.gromacs.org/c/gromacs/+/13886/1/src/gromacs/taskassignment/decidesimulationworkload.cpp#62). At the very least it needs a different name if the only reason for existence is to avoid using an || (e.g. doPmeSpreadOnGpu). _  _ I think a future in which we have both of _  _  PmeRunMode SimulationWorkload::pmeRunMode() const_ _  bool       SimulationWorkload::pmeSpreadOnGpu() const_ _  _ is sound. But if we want to end up there, and avoid verbose || statements in the meantime, we could make the pmeSpreadOnGpu method right now. That's probably a drop-in replacement for what we are currently doing with useGpuPme in do_md and do_force
gromacs~master~I66233f1c790fc5092fb1babaed2ec3ebf16416de;03ab56d1d03ef07a915ad58b3a3b67f47096ee9b;src/gromacs/taskassignment/decidesimulationworkload.cpp;66;0;0;    simulationWorkload.useGpuPmeFft              = (pmeRunMode == PmeRunMode::Mixed)_;> We've already demonstrated why useGpuPme is not easy to use/understand  _  _ Frankly that's just a demonstration of tiredness and stress. _  _ Otherwise, I'm not sure PmeRunMode should be the concern of any code except the internals of PME (and of course some command line parsing as we expose it). Not even task scheduling code needs ATM any awareness of the "Mixed" mode and even if it did, I'd say that's best queried from PME (at least until . Therefore, PmeRunMode should not even be what initializes the workload data, but in principle it should rather be the simulationWorkload that initializes or be followed by the PmeRunMode initialization (and whether it is ::GPU or ::Mixed should can be handled in PME internally).  _  _ > I wouldn't worry about an ||, that shouldn't be more than a cycle. _  _ The point is not so much the || but that we have workload flags as public members now not getters, and last minute design tweaks are far down on my priority list.
