gromacs~master~Ia687929302edd85a0af616b1d947db21e2f3876e;4dde32eea4745325c0ad45aceee2489e190c87df;python_packaging/src/gmxapi/export_context.cpp;84;0;0;        auto                     iterator = py::iter(params["grid"])_;why do you change the loop condition here? I don't understand the pybind stuff much, but I would think that simply iterating over the container is a better approach
gromacs~master~Ia687929302edd85a0af616b1d947db21e2f3876e;4dde32eea4745325c0ad45aceee2489e190c87df;python_packaging/src/gmxapi/export_context.cpp;84;0;0;        auto                     iterator = py::iter(params["grid"])_;The basic C++ iteration seems to work, but to be safe, we ought to check the type of the sequence object and copy it rather than to make assumptions about its iteration behavior. Instead, we can avoid assumptions or explicit typing of the itterable, and directly access the Python iteration behavior this way, which is the only way to iterate on Python objects that is actually documented by pybind. https://pybind11.readthedocs.io/en/master/reference.html#_CPPv48iterator This change seems like a normalizing preventative measure to avoid future surprises, either with changes in Python or pybind, or with gmxapi use cases that we have not yet encountered.
