gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;443109084149ce9abfdd5cda518b22314bd99559;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_force_sender_gpu_impl.cu;107;0;0;    MPI_Send(&pmeSync_, sizeof(GpuEventSynchronizer*), MPI_BYTE, ppRank,;I suspect that this was behind Alan's segfault issue. This needs to be the address of the event (which coincidentally is the address of the struct that holds it), and might have been the address of the unique_ptr when he tried to do that
gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;443109084149ce9abfdd5cda518b22314bd99559;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_force_sender_gpu_impl.cu;107;0;0;    MPI_Send(&pmeSync_, sizeof(GpuEventSynchronizer*), MPI_BYTE, ppRank,;Why are we sending the event/synchronizer every step anyway? The value of the cudaEvent_t never changes after creation. (It's surely implemented as a pointer to an opaque C object.) When you mark an event, the contents of the opaque object change, but not the handle to it (which is the value of the cudaEvent_t), otherwise cudaEventRecord() would have to take a pointer to cudaEvent_t. _  _ So it's enough to make a cudaEvent_t on the PME rank and share that with the PP ranks at setup time. On each step, record the "forces ready" event with it, synchronize with PP ranks using any reasonable method (so they don't enqueue a wait on the event before it is recorded), and only then have the PP rank pull the forces using the force buffer pointer they got at NS time, and the event they got at setup time. They can receive any MPI message e.g. with a suitable tag (ideally with no message payload). _  _ And the synchronization should probably be implemented with an MPI_Irecv posted in advance on the PP side. _  _ (All of that should be an optimization to do later, of course.)
