gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;c7a1283210bbc161f790a819bdae1b911de593ff;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_only.cpp;618;617;618;                if ((pme_pp->pmeForceSenderGpu != nullptr) &&;pme_pp->pmeForceSenderGpu == nullptr  _ when running on CPU. _  _ pme_pp->pmeForceSenderGpu != nullptr _ && _ pme_pp->pmeForceSenderGpu->isGpuCommActiveThisStep() == false _ when running on the GPU and env variable is not active
gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;c7a1283210bbc161f790a819bdae1b911de593ff;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_only.cpp;618;617;618;                if ((pme_pp->pmeForceSenderGpu != nullptr) &&;OK, so if we store a bool in the pme_pp struct as you suggest, there is really no need to also store it in the class, and we can remove the corresponding "set" and "isGpuCommActive" methods (since they just duplicate). This new solution is nicer, because it means we don't need the null checking conditions for the CPU path. I've now done this (and included an assert to check the object exists when the bool is set true).
gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;c7a1283210bbc161f790a819bdae1b911de593ff;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_only.cpp;618;617;618;                if ((pme_pp->pmeForceSenderGpu != nullptr) &&;Sorry, I'm still not quite sure what you mean here. The reason I added the (pme_pp->pmeForceSenderGpu != nullptr) parts is that the pure-CPU run seg-faults without them. The pme_pp->pmeForceSenderGpu->isGpuCommActiveThisStep() call is to look at the boolean flag stored in the class, but this only makes sense for GPU runs which use the CUDA implementation of the class.
gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;c7a1283210bbc161f790a819bdae1b911de593ff;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_only.cpp;618;617;618;                if ((pme_pp->pmeForceSenderGpu != nullptr) &&;We should have a construct along this lines of: _  _   GMX_ASSERT(pme_pp->useGpuDirectComm == (pme_pp->pmeForceSenderGpu != nullptr), "..." )_ _   if ((pme_pp->useGpuDirectComm && _        pme_pp->pmeForceSenderGpu->isGpuCommActiveThisStep())) _   {...}
gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;c7a1283210bbc161f790a819bdae1b911de593ff;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_only.cpp;618;617;618;                if ((pme_pp->pmeForceSenderGpu != nullptr) &&;I don't see why the distinction between where the compute tasks are executed. If compute tasks that produce forces are not on the GPU, we should never have direct communication anyway. _  _ We have three different cases for PME->PP f comm: _ - CPU->CPU (either CPU-only run or manually staged transfers) _ - GPU->GPU or GPU->CPU when the PME on a GPU is the source of the transfer _  _ I think we should use boolean flags that separate these cases and only assert on requiring (pme_pp->pmeForceSenderGpu != nullptr) if the GPU comm is active. _  _ Does that make sense?
gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;c7a1283210bbc161f790a819bdae1b911de593ff;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_pp_comm_gpu_impl.cu;87;85;87;    MPI_Recv(&pmeSync, sizeof(GpuEventSynchronizer*),;This too does not need to be done every step -- the event itself does not get reallocated, the only condition for correctness is that the event has to be recorded before the wait happens, but notifying the receiver does not have to involve resetting pmeSync every step. (not to the future: we should also avoid active receiver notify as other more efficient mechanisms are available that do not block the host)
gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;c7a1283210bbc161f790a819bdae1b911de593ff;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_pp_comm_gpu_impl.cu;87;85;87;             MPI_BYTE, pmeRank_, 0,;This too does not need to be done every step -- the event itself does not get reallocated, the only condition for correctness is that the event has to be recorded before the wait happens, but notifying the receiver does not have to involve resetting pmeSync every step. (not to the future: we should also avoid active receiver notify as other more efficient mechanisms are available that do not block the host)
gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;c7a1283210bbc161f790a819bdae1b911de593ff;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_pp_comm_gpu_impl.cu;87;85;87;    MPI_Recv(&pmeSync, sizeof(GpuEventSynchronizer*),;Ack. Not sure why are you observing what you describe. As a workaround let's stick to the current implementation. However, please do file a redmine so we take a look at this while refining the code after beta. _  _ I'd like us to arrive to a scheme where we don't update pmeSync unless we need to so lifetime of variables is clear. _  _ The solution I have in mind that I was alluding to is that we should be able to use cuStreamWaitValue32 avoiding the WAR hazard on the event itself. Let's look into that after we're done integrating the code.
gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;c7a1283210bbc161f790a819bdae1b911de593ff;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_pp_comm_gpu_impl.cu;87;85;87;             MPI_BYTE, pmeRank_, 0,;Ack. Not sure why are you observing what you describe. As a workaround let's stick to the current implementation. However, please do file a redmine so we take a look at this while refining the code after beta. _  _ I'd like us to arrive to a scheme where we don't update pmeSync unless we need to so lifetime of variables is clear. _  _ The solution I have in mind that I was alluding to is that we should be able to use cuStreamWaitValue32 avoiding the WAR hazard on the event itself. Let's look into that after we're done integrating the code.
gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;c7a1283210bbc161f790a819bdae1b911de593ff;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_pp_comm_gpu_impl.cu;87;0;0;             comm_, MPI_STATUS_IGNORE)_;Seems I independently rediscovered this. at least we agree
gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;c7a1283210bbc161f790a819bdae1b911de593ff;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_pp_comm_gpu_impl.cu;87;85;87;    MPI_Recv(&pmeSync, sizeof(GpuEventSynchronizer*),;IIUC at the minimum, every step we need: _  1) markEvent on PME task _  2) MPI Send/Recv PME->PP to force PP task to wait until event is recorded _  3) enqueueWaitEvent on PP task _  _ At the moment, this is all we have, where in step 2) the MPI Send/recv is of a pointer to the event. In principle we could instead send/recv a single dummy char. We would then need to store the event address as a class private variable, with it being set using a seperate send/recv on NS steps (along with the force buffer address). I had a go at implementing this, but get weird behaviour: the code seg faults right at the end of the run. This is associated with the use of the unique pointer, so something is going wrong when the object (including its new private GpuEventSynchronizer pointer) is being freed (it works OK when using a regular pointer with the "new" syntax). I suggest we just stick with the existing implementation, but if you prefer I will implement the alternative and revert use of the unique pointer.
gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;c7a1283210bbc161f790a819bdae1b911de593ff;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_pp_comm_gpu_impl.cu;87;85;87;             MPI_BYTE, pmeRank_, 0,;IIUC at the minimum, every step we need: _  1) markEvent on PME task _  2) MPI Send/Recv PME->PP to force PP task to wait until event is recorded _  3) enqueueWaitEvent on PP task _  _ At the moment, this is all we have, where in step 2) the MPI Send/recv is of a pointer to the event. In principle we could instead send/recv a single dummy char. We would then need to store the event address as a class private variable, with it being set using a seperate send/recv on NS steps (along with the force buffer address). I had a go at implementing this, but get weird behaviour: the code seg faults right at the end of the run. This is associated with the use of the unique pointer, so something is going wrong when the object (including its new private GpuEventSynchronizer pointer) is being freed (it works OK when using a regular pointer with the "new" syntax). I suggest we just stick with the existing implementation, but if you prefer I will implement the alternative and revert use of the unique pointer.
gromacs~master~I0181ff67065c75f20cddc361f695df9bf888cd88;c7a1283210bbc161f790a819bdae1b911de593ff;src/gromacs/ewald/pme_pp_comm_gpu_impl.cu;87;85;87;    MPI_Recv(&pmeSync, sizeof(GpuEventSynchronizer*),;https://redmine.gromacs.org/issues/3077 created. As described there, it only seems to be an issue when we have cudaStreamDestroy in destructor. I've removed this call for now.
