gromacs~master~Ifc23cc8db2655f7258e68b34e7cdc7b71994e1e8;d5ac016cd15ab9f102192eba09b1ec2ea762ea89;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1543;0;0;                    // Local part of force buffer on GPU must be cleared because halo exchange;At this point, there are no forces in the local part of the rvec-format GPU buffer. There are NB and (potentially) bonded forces in NBAT-format buffer, and PME forces in the new PME staging buffer, and these are all reduced with the forces coming from the halo exchange (which are added to this zeroed buffer in the GPU halo exchange) in the GPU buffer ops. [If there are other forces on the CPU, then instead the above branch is taken.] Does that make sense?
gromacs~master~Ifc23cc8db2655f7258e68b34e7cdc7b71994e1e8;d5ac016cd15ab9f102192eba09b1ec2ea762ea89;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1543;0;0;                    // Local part of force buffer on GPU must be cleared because halo exchange;Yes, that makes sense but is not an elegant and robust solution. OK for now, but the comment should state that this is the GPU rvec buffer and forces calculated on the GPU reside in other GPU buffers.
gromacs~master~Ifc23cc8db2655f7258e68b34e7cdc7b71994e1e8;d5ac016cd15ab9f102192eba09b1ec2ea762ea89;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1543;0;0;                    // Local part of force buffer on GPU must be cleared because halo exchange;Now moved clearing into halo method (see other comments).
gromacs~master~Ifc23cc8db2655f7258e68b34e7cdc7b71994e1e8;d5ac016cd15ab9f102192eba09b1ec2ea762ea89;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1546;0;0;                    nbv->launch_clear_f_on_gpu(Nbnxm::AtomLocality::Local)_;Instead of calling a clearing kernel it would be better to tell the communication kernel to set the local data instead of accumulate into it. This is better for performance and avoids a dependency and a kernel call.
gromacs~master~Ifc23cc8db2655f7258e68b34e7cdc7b71994e1e8;d5ac016cd15ab9f102192eba09b1ec2ea762ea89;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1546;0;0;                    nbv->launch_clear_f_on_gpu(Nbnxm::AtomLocality::Local)_;indeed, I agree -- that's how we do it for the local reduction and the same should be done here too.
gromacs~master~Ifc23cc8db2655f7258e68b34e7cdc7b71994e1e8;d5ac016cd15ab9f102192eba09b1ec2ea762ea89;src/gromacs/mdlib/sim_util.cpp;1546;0;0;                    nbv->launch_clear_f_on_gpu(Nbnxm::AtomLocality::Local)_;It's not quite that simple, because the halo unpacking kernel sprinkles data into the local part of the force buffer, and the untouched elements still need to be zeroed. We need a separate clearing kernel/memset with a whole-GPU grid sync before the halo unpacking kernel. However I have now moved the clearing into the communicateHaloForces method to improve the logic and overlap. It operates in the local stream and overlaps with the halo exchange which operates in the non-local stream. (Then we make the non-local stream wait on the local stream before the unpacking kernel).
